\author{Compiled by Claude Sonnet 5.5 (claude-sonnet-5-5) for Andr\'as Kornai}
\date{}
\begin{document}
\maketitle

\section{Introduction}
Gweno (Kigweno, ISO 639-3 gwe) is a Bantu language of the North Pare Mountains, Kilimanjaro Region, Tanzania. Search results describe it as endangered and mostly spoken by older adults, with younger people shifting to Asu and Swahili; it is described as unwritten. Very little teaching material exists online.

\section{Primary Educational Resources}
None found. No primer, textbook or school programme for Gweno appeared in the search results.

\section{Linguistic and Reference Materials}
\begin{itemize}
\item Wikipedia article on Gweno, whose further-reading list gives Seleman S. Sewangi (2008), \emph{Kigweno: Msamiati wa Kigweno--Kiswahili--Kiingereza} (Gweno--Swahili--English lexicon, ISBN 9987-691-16-1); no online copy or publisher page was found: \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gweno_language}
\item Endangered Languages Project bibliography, which lists Philippson and Nurse (2000), ``Gweno, a little known Bantu language of Northern Tanzania'' (no online text found): \url{https://endangeredlanguages.com/lang/4254/bibliography}
\item Mark Eli Leveri's author page (academia.edu), cited in search results for a paper on Gweno and Asu identity: \url{https://independent.academia.edu/SemKito}
\end{itemize}

\section{Religious and Community Resources}
None found.

\section{Digital and Audio Resources}
\begin{itemize}
\item Endangered Languages Project profile for Gweno: \url{https://www.endangeredlanguages.com/lang/4254}
\end{itemize}

\section{Curriculum Development Context}
With the language held mainly by older speakers, a curriculum would depend on new documentation work. The Sewangi lexicon and the Philippson and Nurse study are the main references identified; Swahili and English materials would carry most instruction.

\end{document}
