\section{Introduction}
Gowro (Gabaro, Gabar Khel, ISO 639-3 gwf) is a small Indo-Aryan (Dardic/Kohistani) language of Kohistan, Pakistan, centred on the village of Mahrin, where Shina is the majority language. Speaker estimates in the sources range from about 200 to about 1,000. Search results show only catalogue-style entries; no teaching materials, grammar, dictionary or recordings were found.

\section{Primary Educational Resources}
None found.

\section{Linguistic and Reference Materials}
\begin{itemize}
\item Wikipedia article on Gowro: \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gowro_language}
\item Verbix endangered-languages entry: \url{https://docs.verbix.com/EndangeredLanguages/Gowro}
\item Search snippets point to Bashir's chapter ``Dardic'' (2003) and Rahman (2006) as scholarly mentions; no online texts were found.
\end{itemize}

\section{Religious and Community Resources}
None found.

\section{Digital and Audio Resources}
\begin{itemize}
\item Endangered Languages Project entry for Gowro: \url{https://www.endangeredlanguages.com/lang/gwf}
\item Foundation for Endangered Languages page on Gowro: \url{https://fli-online.org/gowro/}
\end{itemize}

\section{Curriculum Development Context}
No standard orthography or recent resources were identified. Any curriculum would require primary documentation work with the community, with Shina and Urdu as the surrounding languages of wider communication.

\end{document}
